# Whether a 2-limb value t is a member of an interval with 2-limb bounds (milestone2.md, "One
# enclosure interface", the row of contains(t)): x_l <= t and t <= x_u by le_2_2, which is exact on
# canonical values, and t finite (its leading limb). As for one limb, an infinite t is no member, a
# NaN fails every comparison and the empty set has no member. Exact. For every precision.
kernel:        interval_contains_2
input:         xl = xl0 xl1
input:         xu = xu0 xu1
input:         t = t0 t1
output:        mask m
requires:      interval xl xu
requires:      canonical t
ensures:       certain m member t x x=xl,xu exact
min_precision: 1
source:        the bounds compared with the point by le_2_2, and a test that the point is finite (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        unverified
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   unverified
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the result is a mask, which has no non-finite value, and a comparison with NaN is false; certain states what comes out for every canonical operand

a  = le_2_2(xl0, xl1, t0, t1)
b  = le_2_2(t0, t1, xu0, xu1)
ta = abs(t0)
ft = lt(ta, const inf)
ab = and(a, b)
m  = and(ab, ft)
